# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# netlify.toml serves these headers on Netlify. Cloudflare Pages does not read
# netlify.toml — it reads a `_headers` file from the root of the published
# output directory, which build.js copies this into alongside index.html.
#
# Keeping both means the site can be served from either host, and the move from
# one to the other is a DNS change rather than a code change.
#
# KEEP ALL THREE POLICIES IN SYNC
# The CSP now exists in three places:
#
#   index.html   <meta http-equiv> — covers a locally opened file and any host
#                that serves no headers at all
#   netlify.toml the real header on Netlify
#   _headers     the real header on Cloudflare Pages
#
# A browser given both a meta policy and a header policy enforces the
# INTERSECTION, so tightening one and not the others breaks the app in a way
# that is painful to trace. tests/securityHeaders.test.js fails the suite if
# any two of them disagree on a shared directive — that test is the reason
# three copies is survivable rather than reckless.
#
# EVERY COMMENT LIVES UP HERE, ABOVE THE RULE, ON PURPOSE
# The block below contains header lines and nothing else. A `#` line indented
# inside a rule is accepted by some parsers and treated as the end of the rule
# by others, and the failure mode is not an error — it is a deploy that quietly
# serves fewer headers than it should. Not worth the risk for a tidier layout.
#
# WHAT THE HEADERS ARE FOR
#   Content-Security-Policy   verbatim copy of the netlify.toml policy.
#                             connect-src carries the Supabase REST + realtime
#                             origins and the Open-Meteo endpoint behind the
#                             dashboard weather card; everything else is
#                             same-origin because React, react-dom and
#                             supabase-js are all vendored.
#   X-Frame-Options           frame-ancestors covers modern browsers; this
#                             covers the ones that only understand the legacy
#                             header. SAMEORIGIN matches frame-ancestors 'self'.
#   X-Content-Type-Options    no MIME sniffing.
#   Referrer-Policy           employee IDs and roster views appear in the URL
#                             hash. Send the origin only when leaving the site,
#                             never the path.
#   Permissions-Policy        the portal asks for none of these, so an injected
#                             script cannot ask on its behalf either.
#   Strict-Transport-Security HTTPS only, pinned for a year. No preload — that
#                             is a one-way submission to the browser preload
#                             list and belongs to a deliberate decision about
#                             the domain, not to a headers commit.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co wss://*.supabase.co https://api.open-meteo.com; frame-ancestors 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self';
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
